 - **Fields.** `script` is the only text the voice gets; `transcript` is the same sentences with every equation and number as LaTeX between dollars (what the transcript window shows). `seconds` is a words / 3 estimate until the voice exists, then the file's own length (bytes at 64 kbps). `scriptBy` is the script model; `provider`, `model` and `voiceId` are the voice. The server writes no board: the client lands each answer on the card (`explanation-audio.js` `landNarration`, a quiet update), and the board's normal save registers `narration.fileRef` (`file-refs.js`), so a share or publication link plays it.
-- **Length by Depth.** Brief about 60 to 80 s (180 to 240 words), Balanced about 90 s (240 to 300), Detailed up to about 2 minutes (300 to 360). Soft: the prompt says never pad. The script model is told the card's own Depth (`block.depth`); Gemini gets the higher word block that AUD-2 measured (it undershoots by about a quarter), Fireworks the plain one.
+- **Length by Depth.** Spoken target: Brief about 60 to 80 s (180 to 240 words), Balanced about 90 s (240 to 300), Detailed up to about 2 minutes (300 to 360), stated plainly in the prompt (no "soft", no "never pad", no "stop when the idea is complete"). The script model is told the card's own Depth (`block.depth`). Gemini writes about 0.6 / 0.7 / 0.9 of the words it is asked for (length measurement of 2026-11-17: 144 real calls, raw medians 129 / 205 / 318 words), so its asks are higher (Brief 300 to 360, Balanced 360 to 420, Detailed 340 to 400 words, with a content checklist and "do not stop before N words" for Brief and Balanced); Fireworks gets the plain targets.
+- **Hard floors and the one re-ask (owner 2026-11-17, superseding "no model retry in v1").** After the number check, the kept script must have at least 110 (Brief), 200 (Balanced) or 300 (Detailed) words AND speak at least one of the card's own data numbers (a card with none, or only counting words, asks for no example). A script that fails either gets exactly ONE re-ask: a single extra call to the same model with the same prompt, its first answer, and one user turn that names the failed rule(s) (`too short: N words after removing sentences with outside numbers; target L to H words; use the card's own numbers.` and/or `none of the card's own numbers (...) is spoken; say at least one of them aloud in the explanation`). Still failing after it, the script step is an error (`too_short` or `no_card_number`, the retry pill), never short audio. The answer and the log carry `check` = `{ reasked, firstRawWords, firstStrippedWords, firstFailed?, rawWords, strippedWords, failed }` (the client keeps it as `narration.scriptCheck`); a provider failing on the re-ask hands the card to the next provider, which has no re-ask left.
 - **Style.** The script mirrors the card's own register, vocabulary and rhythm (the prompt's STYLE section); no added enthusiasm.
 - **Prompt.** `explanation-audio-script.js` `scriptSystem` is AUD-2's v1.1 (`docs/features/explain/checks/aud-2/prompt-v1.1.txt`, root causes in `why.md`) with these changes: the numbers rule is the owner's (only numbers that appear in the card or its visuals, never compute; this ends v1.1's tension with its reuse rule); the answer is `{script, transcript}`; Detailed stops at about 2 minutes; the LaTeX delimiter is written by code so the source carries no money-looking text.
-- **Number safety (deterministic, no model).** After the script returns, every number it speaks (digits, and spelled-out words such as "zero point three six eight") must be a number of the card (its prose, the learner's question, every label and value inside its diagrams, in digits or words). A sentence that speaks any other number, or a script sentence that carries a symbol (`= + * / ^ < > [ ] %` and the like), is removed from the script and the transcript together (paired by position when they have the same sentence count; else each on its own). No regenerate, no retry. A lone "one" is not read as a number (it is also the pronoun). A script left under 40 words is an error (`too_short`): no audio worth voicing. Measured offline on AUD-2's real model outputs (the build README): Gemini V6 loses at least one sentence in 4 of 9 scripts (mean 1.2 of 12.4 sentences, 220 to 195 words), Fireworks V5 in 7 of 9 (2.4 of 14.2, 290 to 245 words).
-- **Money.** The script is platform-funded work the learner did not press for: usage feature `explanation_script` (in `FREE_FEATURES`: recorded, never charged), priced at the registry's Gemini or Fireworks rows by `spendTransport`. The voice is the learner's own Play: usage feature `explanation_audio` (charged, `would_charge`), admitted by `elevenSpendTransport` (`learn-paid-transport.js`) at the registry row `elevenlabs | eleven_flash_v2_5 | input_chars` (an estimate row: the list price is per 1,000 characters and the bill is plan credits), behind the paid confirmation (`paidRefusal`: the press sends `confirmed: true`). About 0.3 cent for a script and 1.7 to 7 cents for a voice of 430 to 1,800 characters.
+- **Number safety (deterministic, no model).** After the script returns, every number it speaks (digits, and spelled-out words such as "zero point three six eight") must be a number of the card (its prose, the learner's question, every label and value inside its diagrams, in digits or words). A sentence that speaks any other number, or a script sentence that carries a symbol (`= + * / ^ < > [ ] %` and the like), is removed from the script and the transcript together (paired by position when they have the same sentence count; else each on its own). No regenerate, no retry. A lone "one" is not read as a number (it is also the pronoun). **Counting words** (owner 2026-11-17): when the card has any number, the spelled-out whole numbers two to ten standing alone ("two paths", "the other three") are not outside numbers; ordinals, twice, half, double, pair, a third and a quarter are not number words at all. Data stays counted: digits, decimals, percentages, spelled-out data ("zero point three six eight", "thirty seven", "twelve") and anything 11 and above. A card with no numbers at all follows the old rule (every spoken number is outside). The prompt says to use only the card's own numbers for examples; the rule lets invented small example sizes ("ten emails") pass, which the measurement showed (it saves about 5 words a Gemini script). The Fireworks fallback is also told: no symbols, LaTeX or Greek letters in the spoken script. Measured offline on AUD-2's real model outputs (the build README): Gemini V6 loses at least one sentence in 4 of 9 scripts (mean 1.2 of 12.4 sentences, 220 to 195 words), Fireworks V5 in 7 of 9 (2.4 of 14.2, 290 to 245 words).
+- **Money.** The script is platform-funded work the learner did not press for: usage feature `explanation_script` (in `FREE_FEATURES`: recorded, never charged), priced at the registry's Gemini or Fireworks rows by `spendTransport`. The voice is the learner's own Play: usage feature `explanation_audio` (charged, `would_charge`), admitted by `elevenSpendTransport` (`learn-paid-transport.js`) at the registry row `elevenlabs | eleven_flash_v2_5 | input_chars` (an estimate row: the list price is per 1,000 characters and the bill is plan credits), behind the paid confirmation (`paidRefusal`: the press sends `confirmed: true`). About 0.3 cent for a script (twice that when the re-ask runs) and 1.7 to 7 cents for a voice of 430 to 1,800 characters. The first Play asks first: a confirmation (`ConfirmDialog`, "Make the audio", Cancel and Generate) says "This uses paid generation. Estimated cost: N AI units" with the shared `usageLine` wording; N is the server's `voice_estimate_credits` (registry row, rounded up) kept on the card as `narration.estimate_credits`, and without it the dialog says "Price not available yet" and offers no Generate. A card whose script step failed makes the free script first and then asks.
 - **Guards.** Per-user hour and day caps (`admitUsage`, categories `explanation_script` 60 / 200 and `explanation_audio` 30 / 100; a worker var of the same name, for example `EXPLANATION_AUDIO_HOUR`, overrides one) counted only when a real call is about to be made; the platform spend guard and the learner's allowance (`admitSpend`); owner-only access (`authorizedBoardApp` then `subscriptionOwnerRefusal`), so a visitor of a shared or published canvas has no route to either call and the pill draws only for a ready narration (`ExplanationHost.narrate` exists only where the card can be edited).
-- **Idempotency.** One script per (org, canvas, card id) in the isolate, and two requests in flight share one call. The voice is kept as a file source of the board's owner under a deterministic id from (org, canvas, card id, script hash): a second Play, or a click while it is generating, finds the file or shares the in-flight call and is not billed twice. A retry runs only the step that failed (the pill's error state; a failed voice keeps its script).
+- **Idempotency.** One script per (org, canvas, card id) in the isolate, and two requests in flight share one call. The voice is kept as a file source of the board's owner under a deterministic id from (org, canvas, card id, script hash): a second Play, or a click while it is generating, finds the file or shares the in-flight call and is not billed twice. Across isolates (two tabs) a 15-second in-flight marker per file id (one `shared_ask_events` row, category `explanation_audio_inflight`, inserted atomically only when none is younger than 15 s; the paid routes keep no KV and use D1 for coordination) makes the second Play wait, polling the saved file about every second for up to 20 s, then return it; a failure releases the marker, and a marker with no file after 20 s answers `in_progress`. Rows are not pruned. A retry runs only the step that failed (the pill's error state; a failed voice keeps its script).
 - **Failure never reaches the card.** A provider failure, a refused admission, a missing key (`not_configured`) or no storage is an answer with `status: 'error'` and a short `reason`; the pill shows its existing failed state and a notice says why (`toast`). Logs (`explanation_audio`) carry step, outcome, provider, word and character counts, never card text.
-- **Not built (v1, owner decisions):** an overlap check, a verifier or fact check, a model retry or regenerate, a script edit, other voices, regenerating a card's audio, audio for a card that already exists (only a new card asks for a script), and a cross-isolate lock for the voice call (two tabs pressing Play within the same seconds can bill twice; the file id keeps one file). An old narration with `status: 'failed'` still draws nothing.
+- **Not built (v1, owner decisions):** an overlap check, a verifier or fact check, a model retry other than the one bounded re-ask above, a regenerate, a script edit, other voices, regenerating a card's audio, audio for a card that already exists (only a new card asks for a script). An old narration with `status: 'failed'` still draws nothing.
